abstract: >-
  Tests whether capture-recapture (multiple-systems estimation) over several automated label-error detectors can
  estimate the total number of wrong answer keys in a benchmark, including those no detector found. Two pre-registered
  experiments on MMLU-Redux (181 expert-confirmed key errors) and 200 sets of 266 planted errors. With 2024-era LLM
  judges the detectors flagged most of the benchmark and found 94.5% of errors, so the estimate (181.9) had nothing to
  add: NO-GO. With current open judges and a 5% flagging budget the detectors missed 45%; the estimator's intervals
  covered the truth in 97% of trials but were too wide, and its point estimates further from the truth than the raw
  count: PARTIAL. Also finds that current LLM judges miss about a third of known errors, that confident learning
  out-performs LLM judges, and that planted errors are far easier to detect than real ones.
keywords:
  - label errors
  - benchmark auditing
  - capture-recapture
  - multiple-systems estimation
  - LLM-as-a-judge
  - confident learning
  - MMLU
  - negative result
  - pre-registration
